# The Paper Lantern — visual design

Direction source: director `brief.md`, `script.md`, `delivery-spec.md` at 88905c490117968924467e867110860e21f985ad. Objective: production-designer #1. These designs support the complete five-shot river journey within the director's 20.5-second film.

## Style and lighting

Fine textured watercolor paper; translucent watercolor atmosphere under selective opaque gouache strokes. Soft broken painted edges, no heavy ink outlines, no photorealistic rendering. Quiet blue-violet and muted teal water, dusty mauve at dusk, amber as the sole strong warm accent. Light darkens across shots 1 through 5. The river reflects the stars in the last shot: the lantern stays afloat, without becoming a flying lantern.

## Child

About seven; short straight dark-brown bob, rounded nape. Mustard ochre long-sleeved sweater; dark teal trousers; brown shoes. No hat, bag, jewelry or patterned clothing. Natural child proportions, small hands, simple silhouette. The main views are three-quarter rear in shot 1, hands and sleeves in shot 2, rear view in shot 4. Keep hair length, sweater colour and body proportions fixed. Avoid a facial close-up that would create a second identity.

## Lantern

One small square upright cuboid of ivory rice paper with thin dark brown wooden edge ribs, open top, warm orange-gold glow, on a slightly wider flat dark wood floating tray. No handle, string, balloons or cylindrical body. Its width is approximately the child's two palms. It is held above the river in shot 1, floats independently of the opening hands in shot 2, then recedes downriver. Its warm reflection sits directly beneath it. At tiny scale in shots 4–5, preserve warm colour and subtly square silhouette where pixels allow.

## Place

Old broad stone steps at the village water's edge; slow river curves away toward distant layered indigo hills. Whitewashed cottages with blue slate pitched roofs and a few amber windows, one soft willow silhouette. A timeless modest riverside village, without signs, vehicles or electric street lamps. Riverscape opens toward the horizon as the lantern travels away.

## Cards

Opening plate: empty dusk sky/water in the same painted texture, sufficiently quiet for centered “The Paper Lantern”. End plate: dark blue night sky with sparse stars and no lantern focal point, suitable for “The End”. Typography and timing belong to editor under director's script.

## Acceptance checks

The five stills must show the same child/lantern and physical release, warm windows during the village passage, and a tiny river light among stars. Inspect each raster and the beginning/end framing of every camera move. No embedded text, watermarks, borders or visible anatomy defects. No source or final film is claimed inspected until entered in picture/checks.md.

## Screening revision: release and starfield ending

Director request #4 follows first-time viewing of cut-v1 and quality review of cut-v2. The extended hands in shot02 were ambiguous. Revised shot02 brings both hands back close to the child's chest, elbows bent against the body, while a substantial water gap and trailing ripples separate the floating lantern. The camera follows it away from the child.

In revised shot05 the river widens and its distant surface merges with the sky through mist. The central horizon is open: hills and banks stay at the extreme edges. The only warm point is the lantern near the meeting of sky and river; nearby cool stars and point-like reflections surround it on all sides. There are no competing lit windows or warm shore reflections in this final view. A small push ends on this star-like light. The lantern remains a distant floating light, with no change to its physical design; the transition is pictorial, not flight.
